<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Medang Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">732 CE – 1016 CE (approx.) · capital Mataram (later Tamwlang and Watugaluh)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Javanese kingdom of Mataram that built Prambanan and shared Central Java with the Buddhist Sailendras before moving its court to East Java.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription">Canggal inscription</a> of 732 records King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjaya-of-mataram">Sanjaya</a> erecting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> lingam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, and later kings traced their line to him. The kingdom, called Medang i Bhumi <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a>, grew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedu-plain">Kedu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kewu-plain">Kewu</a> basins around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a>, where the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sailendra-kingdom">Sailendra Kingdom</a> also held power in the late 8th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through rakai, regional lords, and drew revenue from wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming recorded in hundreds of land-grant charters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist shrines stood side by side: the Shaivite Prambanan complex was consecrated in 856 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a>, around 900, ruled a wide realm. In 929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-sindok">Mpu Sindok</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, perhaps after eruptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Merapi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1016 the palace of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa Teguh</a> was sacked by Haji Wurawari, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, an event remembered as the pralaya, the great destruction. Dharmawangsa's son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> survived and founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a>, carrying the Javanese royal tradition forward.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/medang-kingdom.jpg" alt="Medang Kingdom" width="320" height="134"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mataram_Kingdom_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>732 CE – 1016 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mataram (later Tamwlang and Watugaluh)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sanjaya, Balitung, Mpu Sindok</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism) and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mataram Kingdom, Medang i Bhumi Mataram, Medang Mataram</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=874&amp;focus=medang-kingdom" aria-label="Medang Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7888 2498 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Medang Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8208 2754L7994 2708"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7994 2708h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8208 2754h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8208 2754h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mataram. <a href="/atlas?year=874&amp;focus=medang-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
